VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: P16
ATTEMPT: 1
---
Scope: internal/services/storage/storage.go, cache_test.go (commits 06176ef,
79ef006), plus the 2026-08-22 hand merge 2a5c068. All experiments run in a
disposable git worktree at 5eeca01 (`git worktree add ... 5eeca01`), never
touching the tracked repo at /home/darrell/bin/ai/budget2.

What I confirmed holds (criteria 1, 2, 4, 6 from LEDGER_P16_P18_SPEC.md):

1. Reverted the fix to its exact pre-06176ef shape (removed only the
   post-atomicWrite `s.invalidateCache(path)` call in writeFileLocked, leaving
   the pre-write invalidate via encodeForWrite intact -- i.e. "invalidate
   before staging, never touch it again", the bug as described).
   TestReadFileConcurrentWriterNeverServesStale failed at round 4 under
   `-race`: `ReadFile returned "BBBB" after WriteFile("AAAA") returned; the
   cache is serving stale data`. Confirms the bug is real and the fix's
   mechanism (the post-write invalidate) is what closes it.

2. Removed `s.cacheGen++` from `invalidateCache` only (left Lock's own bump).
   Three tests failed: TestReadFileConcurrentWriterNeverServesStale (round
   50), TestPublishCacheRejectsEntryOvertakenByWrite, and
   TestRemoveOrdersCacheAgainstUnlink. The counter is load-bearing, not
   decoration.

3. Removed only Lock's `s.cacheGen++` (map clear left in place).
   TestLockBarsPublishFromReadStartedBeforeLock failed on both counts the
   commit names: "a read started before Lock put plaintext back into the
   cleared cache" and "ReadFile succeeded while the store was locked" --
   matches 79ef006's claim exactly.

4. `go test -race -count=1 ./internal/services/storage/...` on the real
   5eeca01 tree: ok, 220s.

5. Checked encodeForWrite/writeFileLocked, CreateExclusive, removeLocked by
   reading: each calls invalidateCache once before the disk change and once
   after (writeFileLocked's post-atomicWrite call, CreateExclusive's
   post-createExclusive call, removeLocked's own before+after pair). All
   three named write paths are correctly ordered on both sides.

WHAT BREAKS THE CLAIM:

`internal/services/storage/migration.go` (EnableEncryptionWithProvider,
DisableEncryption, and their helpers encryptFileWithRecipient,
decryptFileWithIdentity, rollbackEncryptionWithIdentity) rewrite files in
place at the exact same paths ReadFileContext caches -- via raw
os.WriteFile+os.Rename / os.WriteFile, entirely bypassing
Storage.invalidateCache. None of these five functions call invalidateCache,
delete from s.cache, or touch cacheGen. This is the same file the P16 note
for the second lane names as merged by hand and directs auditing for exactly
this shape of gap ("ask whether any write path reaches disk through a code
path that skips the generation bump") -- and migration.go is such a path.

Reproduced with a scratch test (TestZZMigrationDoesNotBumpGeneration, run
with `-race`, deleted after the run, never landed in the tracked repo):

  - Cached a plaintext ReadFile of ledger.csv (gen=2, entry present).
  - Called s.EnableEncryption(...), which rewrites the same path on disk via
    encryptFileWithRecipient. Result: cacheGeneration() still == 2, and the
    pre-encryption plaintext entry is STILL present in s.cache keyed to that
    path ("cache entry for path still present after EnableEncryption: true").
  - Called s.DisableEncryption(...), which rewrites the path again via
    decryptFileWithIdentity. cacheGeneration() again unmoved.
  - Direct proof of the user-visible consequence, same technique the shipped
    tests use (os.Chtimes to a frozen mtime to model coarse/frozen-mtime
    filesystems, matching WriteFile's own doc comment): sampled gen, did an
    equal-length in-place os.WriteFile + Chtimes-to-frozen rewrite of the
    kind migration.go performs, then called publishCache with the pre-rewrite
    bytes and the sampled gen. It INSTALLED -- "publishCache installed a
    pre-migration-shaped entry ("AAAA") after an equal-length in-place
    rewrite -- because nothing bumped cacheGen for that rewrite, gen was
    still valid." (This step used a bare os.WriteFile to stand in for
    migration.go's own writer, since migration.go's real writer is
    unreachable from the test package boundary without more scaffolding; the
    generation-counter proof in the two steps above -- gen genuinely
    unmoved across two real EnableEncryption/DisableEncryption calls -- is
    the load-bearing evidence and needs no stand-in.)

Consequence: the cache-hit fast path in ReadFileContext
(`s.cacheMu.RLock(); if entry, ok := s.cache[path]; ok && entry.modTime==...
&& entry.size==...`) never touches s.mu and is gated only on the entry still
being present with matching (modTime, size) -- not on cacheGen at all, hits
skip the generation check entirely and rely solely on invalidateCache having
deleted the entry. Because migration.go's five write paths never call
invalidateCache, a stale pre-migration entry is not merely vulnerable to a
generation race, it is never removed from the map at all. It survives until
some other operation (a WriteFile/Remove/CreateExclusive to the same path)
happens to invalidate it. On the coarse/frozen-mtime filesystems the fix's
own doc comment names (WSL2 9p, some network mounts, CI sandboxes), or simply
whenever the pre- and post-migration file sizes coincide, a read immediately
after EnableEncryption/DisableEncryption can serve pre-migration bytes
indefinitely -- the exact bug class 06176ef claims to have closed everywhere,
reachable through a door the fix did not seal.

This is inside the stated scope: the ledger note explicitly assigns the
second lane to audit this file's other write paths for a skipped generation
bump, and migration.go's writers are in the same package, share s.cache and
cacheMu, and were not touched by 06176ef/79ef006/2a5c068. None of the new
tests in cache_test.go exercise EnableEncryption or DisableEncryption.

Criterion 3 ("Both sides of every mutation invalidate") is satisfied for the
three paths it names (atomic replace, CreateExclusive, Remove) but the P16
claim is broader than those three paths, and the second-lane note asks the
broader question directly. On that broader question the answer is no.
